			<div class="content" id="post-content-4820676">
									

<p>Thank you for your unusually kind and thoughtful response. You have a gift for thoughtful writing, it seems. Some unsorted thoughts:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>Given that your user page says, &quot;Oh, hey, I'm [occupation] apparently! That's cool, I hope I'm good at it,&quot; I thought you were writing the dissociation from experience. It's impressive to learn that you weren't.</li>
<li>I've tried workshopping a tale in my head. I have more than enough different things I could write (letters, diary entries, phone calls, streams of thought, excerpts from reports on chemicals, conversations with imaginary therapists, etc.), but it's hard to determine the appropriate amount and variety to use, because I have no native understanding of what parts of dissociation are interesting to other people and what aren't. My experience is that it all is, to an extent, but I could write dissociative stuff endlessly, and I'm sure it gets boring to read at some point. (Probably somewhere around the length of SCP-3999.) I assume it's possible to construct an engaging narrative of arbitrary length from it, but the only inspiration I have for a narrative is chronicling how Dr Rydderech's perspective on reality changes over time, and that's not really a story. Or, it doesn't feel like a story to me; it feels like an autobiography.</li>
<li>I was aware of the lesson of the article when reading it… or, at least, became aware of it some time between reading it and writing my review. Nevertheless, the fact is that SCP-5520 is similar to me, so regardless of authorial intent, it was possible to apply the story's message to myself, and evaluate it on those terms. And while this is no fault of yours, it <em>is</em> representative of a very protective attitude towards continuity of identity that leads most people to be very scared of dissociation and identity death, which seems unwarranted to me. (Not that I'm necessarily right about that.) One way or the other, it's my own responsibility to reject death, and my reaction to this article is at most an iota in my constant conversation with the idea of what I can expect out of my life. You shouldn't feel responsible for any of that.</li>
<li>I'm not all that different from an elderly person, in some ways. I'm physically weak, thanks to chronic fatigue, and I have a bad essential tremor, thanks to meds. I'm older than my life expectancy, and alive mostly by chance. I've outlived some of my friends, and I am equally afraid of the death of my friends as of my own. My past is extremely distant to me; because of how poorly I remember it, I often say that it feels like I have lived thousands of years. I am not likely to ever grow more as a person or become more mature, though in my case, this has been true since my childhood.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>

