			<div class="content" id="post-content-4821250">
									

<p>I'm glad you read my reply! And replied to it, too.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Further&nbsp;thoughts!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;About&nbsp;stuff!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>If you ever want someone to take a look at anything you write, please by all means give me a shout. Every unique voice is important in collaborative fiction! Especially when you're speaking to personal experience.</p>
<p>The thing about being a politician from Québec is a joke; dude got to my name about eighty years before I did. C'est la vie. I do have personal experience dealing with loved ones losing their sense of self, although my reaction was (and would probably continue to be) the opposite of Dr. Scout's; I never give up hope, and choose support over finality. It seemed appropriate for these two characters to reach the conclusions they did, but it's important, <em>very</em> important for me to emphasize that it's Rydderech's right to choose for himself that matters.</p>
<p>I do have obsessive compulsive disorder, which informs some of the mental tribulations I put my characters through. That doesn't give me context for other people's conditions, but it does put me in the mindset of wanting to explore how our minds work, and what our minds mean to ourselves and others.</p>
<p>I'll be mulling over your comments for some time. I edited the article furiously a few times before and after posting it, to try and make it clear that life is precious and valuable but people have the right to decide what to do with theirs; I actually edited it a bit more after reading your comment, to try and shy away from the idea that the depersonalization was Rydderech's main problem. The main problem is really that he's been sequestered underground, alone and unloved, while someone desperately wants to bring him home. I expect I'll go over it again to tighten that linkage; your comments have been very useful for that. So, you've likely helped me reinterpret my work in a way that will further improve it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Every reader has a claim on stories that reflect their lives, so it's very meaningful for me to hear from someone with such an unlikely connection to the subject matter. Thank you!</p>

